## Switch Expression vs Statement

```java
// Old switch statement (verbose, fall-through risk)
String result;
switch (day) {
    case MONDAY:
    case TUESDAY:
    case WEDNESDAY:
    case THURSDAY:
    case FRIDAY:
        result = "Weekday";
        break;
    case SATURDAY:
    case SUNDAY:
        result = "Weekend";
        break;
    default:
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown day: " + day);
}

// Switch expression — concise, no fall-through, no break
String result = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY -> "Weekday";
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> "Weekend";
    // No default needed for exhaustive enums
};
```

## yield — Multi-Line Cases

```java
int numLetters = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, FRIDAY, SUNDAY -> 6;
    case TUESDAY -> 7;
    case THURSDAY, SATURDAY -> 8;
    case WEDNESDAY -> {
        System.out.println("Wednesday has most letters");
        yield 9;  // 'yield' returns value from block
    }
};
```

## Pattern Matching Switch (Java 21)

```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {}
record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
record Rectangle(double w, double h) implements Shape {}
record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {}

double area = switch (shape) {
    case Circle(var r)         -> Math.PI * r * r;
    case Rectangle(var w, var h) -> w * h;
    case Triangle(var b, var h)  -> 0.5 * b * h;
};

// Guard conditions with 'when'
String classify = switch (number) {
    case Integer i when i < 0  -> "negative";
    case Integer i when i == 0 -> "zero";
    case Integer i when i < 10 -> "small positive";
    case Integer i             -> "large positive";
    case null                  -> "null";
};
```

## Core Optional Operations

```java
// orElse vs orElseGet — critical difference
Optional<User> userOpt = userRepo.findById(id);

// orElse: argument ALWAYS evaluated — bad if createDefaultUser() is expensive
User user = userOpt.orElse(createDefaultUser());  // createDefaultUser() ALWAYS runs!

// orElseGet: supplier only runs when Optional is EMPTY — lazy evaluation
User user = userOpt.orElseGet(() -> createDefaultUser());  // only runs if empty

// orElseThrow: clean null-safety with meaningful error
User user = userOpt.orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("User not found: " + id));

// map: transform if present
Optional<String> email = userRepo.findById(id)
    .map(User::getEmail);  // returns Optional<String>, not String

// flatMap: when transform also returns Optional (avoids Optional<Optional<T>>)
Optional<Order> latestOrder = userRepo.findById(id)
    .flatMap(user -> orderRepo.findLatestByUser(user.getId()));

// filter: keep only if condition is true
Optional<User> activeUser = userRepo.findById(id)
    .filter(User::isActive);
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

```java
// BAD: Optional as parameter — caller must wrap values in Optional unnecessarily
public void sendEmail(Optional<String> email) { ... }  // WRONG
// GOOD: use plain type; validate for null inside if needed
public void sendEmail(String email) { Objects.requireNonNull(email, "email required"); }

// BAD: Optional as field — extra object overhead, not serializable
public class User {
    private Optional<String> nickname;  // WRONG
}
// GOOD: plain nullable field
public class User {
    @Nullable
    private String nickname;
}

// BAD: isPresent() + get() — old-style null check in disguise
if (userOpt.isPresent()) {
    User user = userOpt.get();  // WRONG pattern
    process(user);
}
// GOOD: ifPresent or map
userOpt.ifPresent(this::process);
```

## Optional in Repository Layer

```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    // Spring Data returns Optional — consumer handles absence
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);
    Optional<User> findByUsernameAndActiveTrue(String username);
}

// Service layer — clean chaining
public UserDTO getUserByEmail(String email) {
    return userRepo.findByEmail(email)
        .filter(User::isActive)
        .map(UserDTO::fromEntity)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("User not found or inactive: " + email));
}
```

## Built-In Functional Interfaces

```java
// Function<T, R>: takes T, returns R
Function<String, Integer>  parseLength = String::length;
Function<Integer, String>  intToStr    = Object::toString;
Function<String, String>   pipeline    = parseLength.andThen(intToStr);  // compose

// Predicate<T>: takes T, returns boolean
Predicate<String>  notEmpty = s -> !s.isEmpty();
Predicate<String>  isEmail  = s -> s.contains("@");
Predicate<String>  validInput = notEmpty.and(isEmail);  // AND composition

List<String> valid = emails.stream()
    .filter(validInput)
    .toList();

// Supplier<T>: no input, produces T
Supplier<List<String>>  emptyList = ArrayList::new;
Supplier<Instant>       now       = Instant::now;

// Consumer<T>: takes T, no return
Consumer<String>  log   = System.out::println;
Consumer<String>  save  = this::saveToDb;
Consumer<String>  both  = log.andThen(save);  // run both

// BiFunction<T, U, R>: two inputs, one output
BiFunction<String, Integer, String>  repeat = (s, n) -> s.repeat(n);
```

## Method References

```java
// Instance method on type: ClassName::methodName
Function<String, String>  upper = String::toUpperCase;
list.stream().map(String::toUpperCase).toList();

// Instance method on object: instance::methodName
UserService service = new UserService();
Consumer<Long>  findUser = service::findById;  // captures 'service'

// Static method: ClassName::staticMethodName
Function<String, Integer>  parseInt = Integer::parseInt;

// Constructor: ClassName::new
Function<String, User>  createUser = User::new;  // User(String name) constructor
```

## Custom Functional Interface (Checked Exceptions)

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface ThrowingFunction<T, R> {
    R apply(T t) throws Exception;

    static <T, R> Function<T, R> wrap(ThrowingFunction<T, R> f) {
        return t -> {
            try { return f.apply(t); }
            catch (Exception ex) { throw new RuntimeException(ex); }
        };
    }
}

// Usage: wrap checked exceptions for use in streams
List<UserDTO> users = ids.stream()
    .map(ThrowingFunction.wrap(id -> userClient.fetchUser(id)))  // fetchUser throws IOException
    .toList();
```
